<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="configuration" title="Configuration">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Needs a driver already weaving — <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a>. A
            Maven build takes <a href="maven-goals.topic">plugin parameters</a> instead.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        The engine will tell you which layer set every value it runs with. You change no code to
        ask. Every setting is one <code>aether.weaver.</code> key, and the last layer to name it
        wins.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="bash">
java -Daether.weaver.explain=true \
     -Daether.weaver.verification=off \
     -javaagent:lib/agent.jar=verification=report \
     -cp "app.jar:lib/*" app.Main
    </code-block>

    <p>The first thing that run prints is the resolved configuration.</p>

    <code-block lang="console"><![CDATA[
Aether Weaver %version% — plan 48334c30a6d625a7
Configuration: enabled, verification=report, onError=fail, phase=default, explain
  enabled            ← default
  verification       ← agent arguments
  onError            ← default
  phase              ← default
  tags               ← default
  dump               ← default
  explain            ← system properties
  policy.allowSigned ← default
]]></code-block>

    <chapter title="Which layer decides, under the agent" id="layers">
        <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["System properties, read first"] --> C["Stack folded, bottom upwards"]
    B["Agent arguments, added above"] --> C
    C --> D{"Did a layer mention it?"}
    D -->|yes| E["Highest one to speak decides"]
    D -->|no| F["Built-in default applies"]
    E --> G["One configuration, now fixed"]
    F --> G
    G --> H["Weaves discovered, transformer installed"]
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Silence is not a value. A layer that left a key out decides nothing about it, so the
            layer below still counts. That is why <code>verification=off</code> above lost: the
            argument spoke about it, and arguments sit higher.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>Every key resolves once, before a single class is offered. Setting a system
                property later in the run reaches nobody.</p></li>
            <li><p>Two things merge rather than override: the per-weave and per-injection
                overrides, and a relaxed policy.
                <a href="configuration-keys.topic#layers">Configuration keys</a> has both rules.
                </p></li>
            <li><p>Only the agent stacks layers. The weaving class loader is handed a
                configuration already resolved.</p></li>
            <li><p>The full list of keys, their values and their defaults is in
                <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration keys</a>.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <procedure title="Change a setting and prove which layer decided it" id="prove">
        <step>
            <p>Write the key on whichever source you control. On the agent flag the prefix is
                implied and the pairs are comma-separated.
                <a href="agent-options.topic">Agent options</a> has the string.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash">
-javaagent:lib/agent.jar=verification=report
            </code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Add <code>explain=true</code> on any layer and start the application. The console
                block above is that report. One arrow per attributed setting names the layer that
                decided it.</p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Find your key among the eight. Nothing else is attributed, so a
                <code>weave[…]</code> or <code>injector[…]</code> key never appears here, and
                nor does <code>policy.allowPackage</code>.</p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Read the arrow. <code>← default</code> usually means nothing you wrote was accepted,
                so go to <a href="#nothing">When the key you set changes nothing</a>.</p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>An arrow naming a layer you did not expect is the answer. Something else sets that
                key. Remove it there rather than writing the key twice.</p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Drop <code>explain=true</code> again. The closing line still carries the values,
                and <a href="diagnosing-a-weave.topic">Diagnosing a weave</a> reads the rest of
                the report.</p>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <chapter title="Switch one weave off" id="one-weave">
        <p>
            Two settings decide which weaves reach the plan at all. One names a weave class; the
            other reads the tags a weave declares.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
java '-Daether.weaver.weave[app.Audit].enabled=false' \
     -javaagent:lib/agent.jar -cp "app.jar:lib/*" app.Main

java -Daether.weaver.tags.include=audit \
     -javaagent:lib/agent.jar -cp "app.jar:lib/*" app.Main
]]></code-block>

        <warning>
            <p>
                Quote the first one. The brackets are a filename pattern. zsh refuses the word
                outright, and bash passes it on only while no file matches it.
            </p>
        </warning>

        <p>
            The demo declares a single weave. Both commands here drop it, and the closing line
            says so. The second carries the filter that did it.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="console"><![CDATA[
Aether Weaver %version% — no weaves to apply (enabled, verification=strict, onError=fail, phase=default).
Aether Weaver %version% — no weaves to apply (enabled, verification=strict, onError=fail, phase=default, tags=TagFilter[include=[audit], exclude=[]]).
]]></code-block>

        <p>
            Two tags need a system property, because a comma separates agent arguments rather than
            values. <a href="agent-options.topic">Agent options</a> has that split, and
            <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> has what the tags come from.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="A file, if your own code loads one" id="file">
        <p>
            No driver opens a file for you. A properties layer exists where your launcher builds
            one. It sits under the system properties.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="properties" src="weaver.properties"/>

        <code-block lang="java" src="Configured.java" include-lines="21-38"/>

        <code-block lang="console"><![CDATA[
$ java -cp "classes:lib/*" \
       -Daether.weaver.onError=fail launcher.Configured
enabled=true  <- default
verification=report  <- weaver.properties
onError=fail  <- system properties
phase=default  <- default
tags=TagFilter[all]  <- default
dump=none  <- default
explain=false  <- default
policy.allowSigned=false  <- default
woven
hello
]]></code-block>

        <p>
            The loop over <code>layers.settings()</code> prints one line per attributed setting,
            here six of them defaults. Your own code draws those arrows, so they read
            <code>&lt;-</code> where the agent prints <code>←</code>. The loader that takes
            <code>config</code> is in <a href="runtime-weaving.topic">Runtime weaving</a>.
        </p>

        <warning>
            <p>
                Keys in a file carry <code>aether.weaver.</code> in full. One written without it
                is <code>AW2310</code> and is ignored — add the prefix. System properties are the
                exception. An unprefixed one there belongs to somebody else, and is passed over.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="When the key you set changes nothing" id="nothing">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>What you see</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
                <td>The rule</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The key does nothing</td>
                <td><code>AW2310</code> offers a nearer key; use it</td>
                <td><a href="configuration-keys.topic#keys">The keys</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The value does nothing</td>
                <td><code>AW2310</code> offers none; check the value</td>
                <td><a href="configuration-keys.topic#bad-values">A value it cannot read</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>In the report, no effect</td>
                <td>check that this driver reads it</td>
                <td><a href="configuration-keys.topic#drivers">What each driver acts on</a></td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </chapter>

